course: dictionary
lesson: 3462
title: One word, many meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Fair is a word with many meanings, so the words around it tell you which one is meant. «In a fair test you change only one thing and keep everything else the same.» Here fair means equal and right, because every part of the test must be treated the same.
explain: Look for clues. Words like test, rules, share or play tell you about fairness. Words like rides, games and food tell you about a fair, the big outdoor event. Words like hair, skin and light tell you about colour.
explain: Try your meaning in the sentence. «A fair rule is the same for everyone.» If you put "equal and right" in place of fair, the sentence still makes sense, so you have found the right meaning.
fact: Fair can be a noun, an adjective or an adverb. The meaning depends on how it is used.
fact: Fair means equal and right for everyone, following the rules. «A fair rule is the same for everyone.»
fact: A fair is a big outdoor event with rides, games, animals and food. A book fair or a science fair is a different kind of event held at school.
fact: Fair hair or skin is light in colour. Fair weather is fine and dry.
fact: Fair can also mean quite good but not great, as in a fair attempt.
exercise: «A fair rule is the same for everyone.» What does fair mean here? | A big outdoor event with rides | Quite good but not great | Light in colour | *Equal and right for everyone
exercise: The class visited the school science fair. What is a fair in this sentence? | *A big event with rides, games and food | Equal shares for everyone | Light-coloured hair | Dry, sunny weather
exercise: Ella says, «Make sure everyone gets a fair share of the cake.» What does fair share mean? | A share that is wet and cold | The biggest slice of the cake | *An equal part for each person | A share that is light in colour
test: «The fair weather stayed all week, so we played outside every day.» What does fair mean here? | Light in colour | *Fine and dry | Equal and right for everyone | A big event with rides
test: «Her fair hair shone in the sunlight.» What does fair mean here? | A big event with rides | *Light in colour | Quite good but not great | Equal and right for everyone
exam: Jayden says fair always means the same as good. Is he right? | Yes, fair always means good | No, fair can mean equal, a big event, light in colour, fine weather or quite good | *No, fair has several meanings, and the context shows which one is meant
